{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PkoenBuut6pKxYktik92Dy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# warmups — technique log (days 1-18; updated after every day)\n2\t\n3\tHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n4\t- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n5\t  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n6\t- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n7\t- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13, sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal, \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL percent-encoding, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML character entities, \"characters would like to escape\"/\"Escape Room\" -> JSON/JS `\\uXXXX` string escapes, \"Thirty-Two Below\" -> base32, \"URL-friendly vacation\"/\"Safe Travels\" -> URL-safe base64 with the `=` padding DROPPED, \"Maybe we started at the wrong end\"/\"Back at You\" -> the plain sentence REVERSED\n8\t  (character order, no other encoding), \"A met Z. They really hit it off.\"/\"Opposite Day\" -> ATBASH (a<->z mirror), \"Caesar took a week off.\"/\"Seven Steps\" -> Caesar +7 (decode rot-7), \"Even the punctuation went for a spin.\"/\"Room\n9\t  Forty-Seven\" -> ROT47 (rotates every printable `!`-`~`, so digits/hyphens/colon change too), \"Sixteen little symbols are\n10\t  calling. Dot dot dot.\"/\"Dot Com\" -> MORSE spelling the HEX digits of the text (TWO layers: Morse outer, hex inner).\n11\t  \"Sixteen guests. Five little a's and b's apiece.\"/\"Bacon Bits\" -> BACONIAN 5-symbol a/b groups whose VALUES (0-15) are\n12\t  the HEX nibbles of the text (Bacon outer, hex inner). D1-D15 single_solves were ONE layer; D16 and D17 are stacked, the\n13\t  pun naming BOTH layers (\"sixteen\" = hex both days; \"dot dot dot\" = Morse, \"a's and b's\"/\"Bacon\" = Baconian). Read every\n14\t  number/word in the prompt as a possible layer; \"Sixteen\" has meant a hex INNER layer twice running.\n15\t  \"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\"/\"Off the Rails\" -> RAIL FENCE, 3 rails, offset 0 (D18, back to ONE layer, the\n16\t  first true TRANSPOSITION after D12's reversal: same characters, new order). The prompt's number was the KEY (rails).\n17\t  Families run in streaks (D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex), so expect more transpositions.\n18\t- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 83, 88). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, `\"\\uXXXX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, plain reversed text, Atbash, Caesar+7, ROT47, Morse-over-hex, Bacon-over-hex, rail-fence-3, in that order so far). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n19\t- Clocks: TT about 5-8 s from day 2 on (30 s on day 1 and again on day 12), mastery about 5-9 s (30 s on day 12; the window does not reliably grow with the set count). Only a deterministic solver survives these windows. The single_solve is untimed.\n20\t- D16 GM regime change: TT/Mastery are \"regular scored challenges by default\" (contract `deadline: null`, no window);\n21\t  `run_for_time: true` on start/retry requests a timed Hall of Fame attempt. The HARNESS still kills solve.py after 60 s\n22\t  (D17 Mastery: `exec_error`, `solver_too_slow`, nothing submitted) -- keep every set well under 1 s.\n23\t  D18 the clocks were back: TT start returned a 30 s deadline, Mastery a 105 s window (88 sets).\n24\t\n25\t## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n26\t- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, `&#NNN;`\n27\t  entities, `\"\\uXXXX\"` JSON escapes, ...); detect each line's encoding from its alphabet, not its position (position-based detection breaks if the order\n28\t  changes). Rule for \"escape-token\" encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind\n29\t  (`re.sub(token,'',frag).strip()==''`), then use the stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) so the unseen forms\n30\t  (`%XX` upper, `&#x6d;` hex, `&amp;` named) are covered too. Day 8 GM bytes used only decimal `&#NNN;` in all 40 blobs.\n31\t  Day 9 backslash escapes: every char as lowercase `\\u00XX`, the fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (a JSON string\n32\t  literal) in all 45 blobs (single, TT, 43 mastery lines); no `\\x`, `\\n`, `\\\"`, uppercase hex or non-ASCII seen. Rule: if\n33\t  the fragment matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, decode with `json.loads` (add the quotes if missing) -- that also covers\n34\t  `\\n`, `\\\"`, surrogate pairs and non-ASCII; fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`. Drop the quotes.\n35\t- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11) -- standard `+/` with `=` (D1) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO `=` (D11): the single was\n36\t  147 chars (len%4==3) and its bytes happened to hold no `-`/`_`; the TT blob was len%4==0 (strict b64 also decodes it);\n37\t  mastery line 11 is 14 chars (`bi1tYXBsZS00OA` -> `n-maple-48`). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad\n38\t  `'='*(-len%4)` and `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` has this branch since D11.\n39\t- **Direction (D12)** -- the text itself can be BACK-TO-FRONT: single/TT = the whole sentence reversed\n40\t  (`56-ztrauq-tesnus-ytsez :retsaM emaG ...`), Mastery line 12 = a reversed slug tail (`69-enud-m` -> `m-dune-96`,\n41\t  `21-teggun`, `36-yvi-ma`). Rot-N \"decodes\" such a line into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`) -- 11/58 Mastery sets went\n42\t  wrong that way. Parse rule (in `lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D12): a free-text fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*`\n43\t  is a reversed slug tail; otherwise take the reversal when it out-scores every rot-N on bigrams. 0 false hits on D3-D11.\n44\t- **Mirror vs shift vs reversal (D13)** -- three FREE-TEXT transforms now coexist in one Mastery blob: rot13 (line 2),\n45\t  reversal (line 12), Atbash (line 13). Chunk edges split words (`-tliuq-d`, `m-uvim-66`, `yirwtv-78` -> `bridge-78`), so\n46\t  per-line bigram guessing confused rev/Atbash on 20/63 sets. Parse rule (D13 solver `_joint`): decode the strict lines,\n47\t  then try every assignment of {rot-N, reversal, Atbash} to the free lines, EACH TRANSFORM USED ONCE per blob, keep the\n48\t  one whose joined text ends in a slug, best bigram. Whole-blob Atbash (single/TT) is in `lib.decode.decode_chunk`.\n49\t  D14 added a FOURTH free codec, a second shift: Caesar +7 on line 14 (`u-pcf-67` -> `n-ivy-67`) beside rot13 on line 2.\n50\t  Same rule, `lib.decode.FREE_CODECS` = rot(2) / rev(12) / atb(13) / caesar7(14); and rank a 3-word `adj-noun-noun-NN`\n51\t  slug end above a 2-word one (set22 swapped rev/Atbash on lines 12/13 and still \"ended in a slug\": `g-voaafk|urtle-60`).\n52\t  D15 added a FIFTH, ROT47 on line 15 (`@AA6C\\eg` -> `opper-68`: hyphen -> `\\`, digits -> `_`,`` ` ``,`a`-`h`, lowercase ->\n53\t  `2`-`K`). It is NOT letters-only, so rot-N/Atbash/reversal can't produce it; registered with\n54\t  `decode.register_free(\"rot47\", 15, ...)` (lib already listed rot47 as a SPECULATIVE free codec, day=None). 73/73 first try.\n55\t  Rule for any new keyless printable-range shift: one `register_free(name, day, fn)` call, never a per-line special case.\n56\t- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16)** -- single, TT and Mastery line 16 were all Morse whose decoded symbols are\n57\t  ONLY `0-9A-F` (hex of the UTF-8 text): symbols split by ONE space, no `/` or 3-space word gaps (hex has no spaces), and\n58\t  Morse is case-free, so the hex comes out UPPERCASE (`bytes.fromhex` takes it). Blob ~11 bytes per plaintext char\n59\t  (1257 B single); a Mastery line-16 fragment = 14 symbols = 7 bytes (`-.... .---- --... -----` = `6170` = `ap`).\n60\t  Parse rule: after any case/charset-collapsing outer decode (Morse, later NATO/Braille/tap...), if the output is\n61\t  `(?:[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+` -> `fromhex`; digits-only tokens -> decimal/octal char codes; otherwise feed it to\n62\t  `decode_chunk` once more; accept only printable ASCII ending in a slug. In lib since D16: `decode_chunk`'s Morse\n63\t  branch returns the hex-decoded text when the Morse output is pure hex pairs (gated `_max_day() >= 16`).\n64\t  D17 = a SECOND hex carrier, Baconian: lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups split by ONE space, only `aaaaa`..`abbbb` occur (values\n65\t  0-15, so every group starts with `a`), TWO groups per byte, ~12 B per plaintext char (1295 B single); a Mastery line-17\n66\t  fragment = 14 groups = 7 bytes. Read as Bacon-26 letters it gives ONLY `a`-`p` (`eogjgd...`) = the a-p hex alphabet\n67\t  (nibble = ord-97) -- the answer-first worker went that way. Parse rule for any two-symbol group carrier: map the 2\n68\t  symbols to 0/1, cut into fixed-width groups, then values all < 16 with an even count -> hex nibbles -> `fromhex`;\n69\t  < 26 -> Bacon-26 letters (24-letter I=J/U=V if that reads better). In lib since the D17 night: `decode_chunk` strict\n70\t  BACON-HEX branch (`[ab]{5}` groups, gated `_max_day() >= 17`), a `warmups_codecs.DAYS` D17 encoder, and the generic\n71\t  `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` (any 2 symbols, widths 4-8, both polarities; also `bin4_dec`, `aphex_dec`, `tern_dec`,\n72\t  `radix_dec`, `chuck_dec`, `dna_dec` for the sibling carriers). The D17 solver also has `register_free(\"baconhex\", 17, ...)`.\n73\t- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same character multiset, new order. Single/TT: the whole 109-110 B sentence\n74\t  rail-fenced, 3 rails, offset 0 (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae...` = rail 0 then 1 then 2; one `!` `,` `:`, slug digits intact but\n75\t  scattered). Mastery line 18: the 6-8 char slug-tail fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (key restarts per line):\n76\t  `p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56` (rail0 = chars 0,4; rail1 = 1,3,5; rail2 = 2,6). A 7-char rail-3 fragment still \"looks like\"\n77\t  slug text, so no strict class claims it and best-rot/reversal would return garbage. Parse rule: for an anagram of the\n78\t  crib (or a fragment no strict branch takes), try `warmups_codecs.rail_dec(t, r, off)` for r=3 first, then r 2..11 x\n79\t  every offset, accept only a crib/slug hit (`Master:\\s*[a-z]+-[a-z]+-[a-z]+-\\d{2}$`); in Mastery try rail on each\n80\t  unclaimed line inside the slug-gated joint search (lib `rail_solve`/`rail_free`, `register_free` with day 18). Any\n81\t  future keyless permutation (columnar width, route, skip) is the same rule: enumerate the keys, gate on the crib/slug.\n82\t- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n83\t- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 78 on day 16, 83 x 17 lines on day 17, 88 x 18 on day 18); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n84\t- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n85\t  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n86\t- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n87\t- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n88\t\n89\t## Recognition cues -> first move\n90\t| What you see | First move |\n91\t|---|---|\n92\t| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n93\t| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n94\t| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n95\t| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n96\t| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n97\t| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n98\t| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n99\t| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n100\t| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n101\t| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n102\t| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n103\t| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n104\t| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n105\t| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n106\t| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n107\t| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n108\t| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n109\t| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n110\t| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n111\t| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n112\t| Magic `1f8b`/`78`/`BZh`/`fd377a58` | gunzip / zlib / bzip2 / xz (in the toolbox. Not seen in the GM bytes yet) |\n113\t| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\n114\tThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n115\t\n116\t## Day by day\n117\t**Days 1-6** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal): one layer each, solved in seconds-minutes.\n118\tLessons that still matter: D1 mastery failed ~12x because same-named `blob.txt` downloads overwrote each other (match by\n119\tsha256); D3 mastery timed out when single letters/hex counted as English (score 2+-letter words, cap peel at 0.6 s); D4 a\n120\trot-N must beat the UNSHIFTED text by a margin (\"er:\" -> \"an:\"); D6 a shell `read` loop dropped the last token (no trailing\n121\tnewline), octal vs decimal = zero padding + lowercase share. Mastery from D2 on = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt`.\n122\t\n123\t**Days 7-9** (URL `%xx`, decimal HTML entities, quoted `\"\\u00XX\"` JSON string): one layer each, CORRECT in 18-53 s.\n124\tLessons that still matter: D7 mastery attempt 1 decoded the WHOLE zip (bundle saved as `blob-01.txt`, a name match\n125\treturned the outer file) -> another set's slug fragment `py-storm-dune-80` -> incorrect; `lib.common.find_artifact` now\n126\treturns the same-named member. D8 a `str.replace` solver patch silently matched nothing (assert anchors). D9 lib's `\\u`\n127\tpath is ASCII-only (`\\u00e9`, `\\n`, `\\\"` would fall through to rot-N garbage; not in GM bytes yet). Strict whole-fragment\n128\t`%xx` and entity branches live in `lib.decode.decode_chunk`.\n129\t\n130\t**Days 10-13** (base32; unpadded base64url; reversed sentence; Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s.\n131\tLessons that still matter: D11 `base64 -d`/strict len%4 b64 refused the unpadded blob and old `decode_chunk` fell through\n132\tto rot-N garbage (re-pad first). D12 the carried-over solver answered in 5 s, but Mastery lost the timed attempt: a 28 s\n133\tgeneric fallback, then 11/58 sets where rot-N \"decoded\" the reversed line 12 (`69-fove-n`). D13 the probe submitted a rot-N\n134\tof Atbash text (`ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31`, rejected: gibberish words); Mastery 39/63 until free lines were assigned\n135\tJOINTLY (`lib.decode._joint_free`, `FREE_CODECS`; each transform used once per blob).\n136\t\n137\t**Days 14-17** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex): CORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day.\n138\tLessons that still matter: D14 Mastery set22 went MISSING when a rev/Atbash swap ended in a 2-word slug -> `caesar7` in\n139\t`FREE_CODECS`, 3-word slug ranked first. D15 ROT47 (`tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O'`) is beyond rot-N/Atbash/reversal;\n140\t`register_free(\"rot47\", 15, ...)`. D16 Morse whose symbols are only `0-9A-F` -> `fromhex` (first two-layer warmup). D17\n141\tBacon groups `aaaaa`-`abbbb` = nibbles; the answer-first worker read Bacon-26 letters (`a`-`p` only) then `ord-97` ->\n142\t`fromhex` (~25 s). D17 Mastery (83 x 17) first run hit the harness's 60 s budget -- `answer()` ran the joint free-codec\n143\tranker on every set even after `decode_chunked` had the slug; return-early fix -> 83 sets in 1.35 s.\n144\t\n145\t**Day 18: Off the Rails** (\"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\" -- rail fence, 3 rails, offset 0).\n146\t- 109-byte blob, no newline, printable ASCII: `N kcnhwuTo,b saaeoemararm-o9iewr eoigti amp ...` = the crib sentence's\n147\t  characters (one `!` `,` `:`) in a new order; triage preview alone shows it is an anagram. Rail 0 holds chars 0,4,8,...\n148\t- Answer-first worker: one `python3 -I -c` that rail-decodes for r in 2..N x every offset and prints the reads; (3,0) gave\n149\t  the sentence (offsets 2/3 give rotated copies `29Nice work...` -- reject those, the slug must END the text). Direct\n150\t  `hctf submit`: start 13:00:09Z -> CORRECT 13:00:24Z (~15 s, first submit). lib already had `warmups_codecs.rail_solve`\n151\t  (the atlas had listed rail fence as \"high: Off the Rails transposition\"), but the carried D17 solver never called it:\n152\t  the prior-solver dry run answered 0/1. The duel worker's solve.py was verified at +41 s.\n153\t- TT: the TT `start` at +19 s opened a 30 s clock (deadline 13:00:55Z) before any solver was verified; the timed run at\n154\t  +47 s got `retry_variants_unavailable` -> untimed fallback, CORRECT (solve 0.17 s), no Hall of Fame.\n155\t- Mastery 88 sets x 18 lines, window 105 s: the today-probe (`compound-today-d18-edge`, rail3 on the last line) caught that\n156\t  `decode_chunked` misses line 18, so `_rail_lines` (each line in turn rail-decoded at (3,0),(2,0),(3,1..3), slug-gated)\n157\t  was added before the attempt; 88/88 first try (solve 0.7 s, in_window 5.77 s).\n158\t\n159\t## GM answer conventions learned\n160\t- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n161\t- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-18; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n162\t- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n163\t- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n164\t\n165\t## Pitfalls that cost time\n166\t- Mastery bytes missing: duplicate `blob.txt` downloads overwrote each other or were never fetched (day 1, about 12 failed attempts). If a set's bytes aren't on disk, it is a downloader problem. Don't spawn solver-hardening workers.\n167\t- A `.txt` file that is really a ZIP (check the magic, not the extension). Prefer the exact inner member over the outer same-named container. Day 7 mastery lost an attempt to this: set 1 decoded the whole bundle. A slug that is a word suffix (`py-...`), or a set1 answer that doesn't come from its own last line, is the tell. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the zip-named-`blob-01.txt` layout, so test on the raw delivery.\n168\t- English scoring: count single letters and hex reads as English (day 3 timeout). Score common 2-letter words without a margin and a correct line gets rot-shifted (day 4). Require a margin over the unshifted text.\n169\t- Encoding confusion: binary is also valid hex (try binary first), and decimal is also valid octal (use padding and case).\n170\t- Fragments are split mid-word. Join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace. Joining with spaces corrupts the sentence and the slug.\n171\t- No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Use python or `tr ' ' '\\n'` plus an explicit newline. Always regex-check the slug against the answer syntax.\n172\t- Timed windows (5-9 s, with GM start/submit eating 3-5 s of it) leave no room for an LLM or a slow BFS. Keep every per-question decode under about 1 s with a hard BFS cap.\n173\t- Solver patches made with `str.replace`/sed silently no-op when the anchor text doesn't match exactly (day 8 fallback). Assert\n174\t  the anchor count or re-grep the file after patching.\n175\t- Hand-testing escape decoders: in a ratchet session, a literal backslash-u sequence typed into a Bash/heredoc command\n176\t  reached python ALREADY decoded (the tool layer unescaped it), so a test \"passed\" on plain text. Test on the artifact file\n177\t  or build escapes with `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n178\t- Unpadded base64: tools that demand `len%4==0` reject it or (worse) a length-gated classifier skips it and a fallback\n179\t  returns plausible-looking rot-N text. Re-pad before decoding; `len%4==1` is the only impossible length.\n180\t- Rot-N is the catch-all of `decode_chunk`, so ANY unrecognised plain-text transform (reversal on D12) comes back as\n181\t  plausible-looking rot garbage, not as a failure. When a new day's line has no strict class, check `[::-1]` and other\n182\t  plain transforms BEFORE trusting rot-N, and verify the slug's words read as English (`fove`, `ulev` don't).\n183\t- Rejected D13 single `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31`: a rot-N of Atbash text. Gibberish slug words = an unknown free-text\n184\t  transform, not a GM mismatch -- don't submit a slug whose words aren't English.\n185\t- Several free-text transforms in one blob (D13: rot13 / reversal / Atbash): decide them JOINTLY with each used once, not\n186\t  per fragment -- short edge-split fragments score ambiguously and cost 24/63 Mastery sets.\n187\t- A synthetic \"today's codec on line N\" check (compound-today cases) passing does NOT prove the joint assignment: D14's\n188\t  real set22 still broke on edge-split rev/Atbash lines. Any fallback path must classify free lines exactly as\n189\t  `lib.decode._free_line` does (rot / reversal / Atbash / new codec), and a slug check should demand the full 3-word shape.\n190\t- The joint free-codec search grows with every new free codec (D15 Mastery solve 3.46 s with 5 free lines vs 0.2-0.3 s on\n191\t  D8-D11). Prune by alphabet first: a ROT47 fragment has non-letters, so test that strict branch before the joint search.\n192\t- A collapsed-alphabet outer layer (D16 Morse) decodes to something that is NOT English (`4E696365...`): a decoder that\n193\t  stops after one layer returns no slug (or, scored as \"wordy\", a junk reading). When a decode yields only hex/digit\n194\t  characters, it is an inner layer: decode again. Prompts with two cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\") = two layers.\n195\t- lib has TWO Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` knows letters/digits only (drops `!` `,` `:` and the slug hyphen\n196\t  `-....-`); `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec` knows punctuation. Use `morse_dec` for plain-text Morse.\n197\t- A slow fallback (generic `peel` BFS per set) x 58 sets blows even a 30 s window: gate fallbacks on a hard per-set cap.\n198\t- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): a solver that builds `[decode_chunked(t)] + decode_candidates(t)` and then picks\n199\t  pays the joint rot/rev/Atbash/... search on EVERY set. At 83 sets x 17 lines that passed 60 s and the Mastery run died\n200\t  with no submission. Return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on a miss. Time the\n201\t  real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new free codec -- the cost grows with sets x lines x codecs.\n202\t- A carrier decode that \"works\" can still be a middle layer: Bacon-26 letters of D17 came out as `a`-`p` only. An output\n203\t  alphabet of exactly 16 symbols (0-9A-F, a-p, 16 words) = hex nibbles; decode again.\n204\t- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18 rail fence): spaces are ciphertext -- a rail can start or end with a space, so strip only `\\r\\n`,\n205\t  never `.strip()`. Wrong offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug (`29Nice work ... dapper-summit-brook-`):\n206\t  accept only a read that STARTS with `Nice work` / ends in a full `adj-noun-noun-NN`. Charset tells you nothing (it is\n207\t  the plaintext's), the cue is \"readable letters, wrong order, exactly one `!` `,` `:`\".\n208\t- A prebuilt lib codec is useless if the carried solver never routes to it (D18: `rail_solve` existed, prior solver 0/1).\n209\t  First move on a new day: run lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` + `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n210\t- Mastery fragments of a keyed/transposition codec restart the key PER LINE and are short (6-8 chars on D18); only a\n211\t  slug/crib gate over the joined text can confirm them. Test today's codec on the last Mastery line (compound-today probe).\n212\t- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n213\t- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n214\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3998553-dd92-4aba-82dd-28852dfc57a2","uuid":"5092ffe1-58a7-4caa-9aef-957e68e5acd2","timestamp":"2026-10-19T13:03:47.271Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/techniques/warmups.md","content":"# warmups — technique log (days 1-18; updated after every day)\n\nHow this lane has looked so far (observations, not guarantees):\n- **Timed variants MUTATE** (organizer, Day 8: \"new, dynamic variations/mutations ... new unseen effects\"): nothing below is a\n  guarantee for Time Trial / Mastery -- solvers derive structure and degrade per field.\n- Each day: one `blob.txt`. Decode it until a plain English sentence appears; its final non-empty line ends \"...submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <slug>\". The slug is `word-word-word-NN` (`answer_format.kind=pattern`, `normalization=trim`). Same single question id `message` every day.\n- The ENCODING changes daily and the title/prompt is a pun naming it: 64 -> base64, \"thirteen steps\"/half a turn -> ROT13, sixteen fingers -> hex, \"two sides\" -> 8-bit binary, \"reduced to numbers\" -> decimal char codes, \"eight fingers\" -> octal, \"address bar\"/\"Percent\" -> URL percent-encoding, \"the browser understands\"/\"Entity\" -> HTML character entities, \"characters would like to escape\"/\"Escape Room\" -> JSON/JS `\\uXXXX` string escapes, \"Thirty-Two Below\" -> base32, \"URL-friendly vacation\"/\"Safe Travels\" -> URL-safe base64 with the `=` padding DROPPED, \"Maybe we started at the wrong end\"/\"Back at You\" -> the plain sentence REVERSED\n  (character order, no other encoding), \"A met Z. They really hit it off.\"/\"Opposite Day\" -> ATBASH (a<->z mirror), \"Caesar took a week off.\"/\"Seven Steps\" -> Caesar +7 (decode rot-7), \"Even the punctuation went for a spin.\"/\"Room\n  Forty-Seven\" -> ROT47 (rotates every printable `!`-`~`, so digits/hyphens/colon change too), \"Sixteen little symbols are\n  calling. Dot dot dot.\"/\"Dot Com\" -> MORSE spelling the HEX digits of the text (TWO layers: Morse outer, hex inner).\n  \"Sixteen guests. Five little a's and b's apiece.\"/\"Bacon Bits\" -> BACONIAN 5-symbol a/b groups whose VALUES (0-15) are\n  the HEX nibbles of the text (Bacon outer, hex inner). D1-D15 single_solves were ONE layer; D16 and D17 are stacked, the\n  pun naming BOTH layers (\"sixteen\" = hex both days; \"dot dot dot\" = Morse, \"a's and b's\"/\"Bacon\" = Baconian). Read every\n  number/word in the prompt as a possible layer; \"Sixteen\" has meant a hex INNER layer twice running.\n  \"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\"/\"Off the Rails\" -> RAIL FENCE, 3 rails, offset 0 (D18, back to ONE layer, the\n  first true TRANSPOSITION after D12's reversal: same characters, new order). The prompt's number was the KEY (rails).\n  Families run in streaks (D12-D15 free-text letter maps, D16-D17 carriers over hex), so expect more transpositions.\n- Mastery is cumulative. Day N mastery = 5N-2 sets (3, 8, 13, ..., 83, 88). Each set blob has N numbered lines `NN<TAB>fragment`, and line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, html-entity, `\"\\uXXXX\"`, base32, unpadded base64url, plain reversed text, Atbash, Caesar+7, ROT47, Morse-over-hex, Bacon-over-hex, rail-fence-3, in that order so far). The last line has no trailing newline. The fragments join verbatim (split mid-word) into the same sentence.\n- Clocks: TT about 5-8 s from day 2 on (30 s on day 1 and again on day 12), mastery about 5-9 s (30 s on day 12; the window does not reliably grow with the set count). Only a deterministic solver survives these windows. The single_solve is untimed.\n- D16 GM regime change: TT/Mastery are \"regular scored challenges by default\" (contract `deadline: null`, no window);\n  `run_for_time: true` on start/retry requests a timed Hall of Fame attempt. The HARNESS still kills solve.py after 60 s\n  (D17 Mastery: `exec_error`, `solver_too_slow`, nothing submitted) -- keep every set well under 1 s.\n  D18 the clocks were back: TT start returned a 30 s deadline, Mastery a 105 s window (88 sets).\n\n## Variant axes (what differs between the single, Time Trial and Mastery variants -- parse every value listed)\n- **Encoding per line** -- Mastery line k uses day k's encoding (b64, rot13, hex, binary, decimal, octal, url-%XX, `&#NNN;`\n  entities, `\"\\uXXXX\"` JSON escapes, ...); detect each line's encoding from its alphabet, not its position (position-based detection breaks if the order\n  changes). Rule for \"escape-token\" encodings (url `%xx`, entities): accept when the WHOLE fragment is tokens of that kind\n  (`re.sub(token,'',frag).strip()==''`), then use the stdlib decoder (`unquote`, `html.unescape`) so the unseen forms\n  (`%XX` upper, `&#x6d;` hex, `&amp;` named) are covered too. Day 8 GM bytes used only decimal `&#NNN;` in all 40 blobs.\n  Day 9 backslash escapes: every char as lowercase `\\u00XX`, the fragment WRAPPED IN DOUBLE QUOTES (a JSON string\n  literal) in all 45 blobs (single, TT, 43 mastery lines); no `\\x`, `\\n`, `\\\"`, uppercase hex or non-ASCII seen. Rule: if\n  the fragment matches `^\"?(?:\\\\(?:u[0-9a-fA-F]{4}|x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}|[nrt\"\\\\/]))+\"?$`, decode with `json.loads` (add the quotes if missing) -- that also covers\n  `\\n`, `\\\"`, surrogate pairs and non-ASCII; fall back to `codecs.decode(t,'unicode_escape')` for `\\xNN`. Drop the quotes.\n- **Base64 alphabet/padding** (D11) -- standard `+/` with `=` (D1) vs URL-safe `-_` with NO `=` (D11): the single was\n  147 chars (len%4==3) and its bytes happened to hold no `-`/`_`; the TT blob was len%4==0 (strict b64 also decodes it);\n  mastery line 11 is 14 chars (`bi1tYXBsZS00OA` -> `n-maple-48`). Rule: `[A-Za-z0-9_-]+` with len%4 in {0,2,3} -> re-pad\n  `'='*(-len%4)` and `urlsafe_b64decode`; len%4==1 is never base64. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` has this branch since D11.\n- **Direction (D12)** -- the text itself can be BACK-TO-FRONT: single/TT = the whole sentence reversed\n  (`56-ztrauq-tesnus-ytsez :retsaM emaG ...`), Mastery line 12 = a reversed slug tail (`69-enud-m` -> `m-dune-96`,\n  `21-teggun`, `36-yvi-ma`). Rot-N \"decodes\" such a line into plausible garbage (`69-fove-n`) -- 11/58 Mastery sets went\n  wrong that way. Parse rule (in `lib.decode.decode_chunk` since D12): a free-text fragment shaped `\\d{2,}-[A-Za-z][A-Za-z-]*`\n  is a reversed slug tail; otherwise take the reversal when it out-scores every rot-N on bigrams. 0 false hits on D3-D11.\n- **Mirror vs shift vs reversal (D13)** -- three FREE-TEXT transforms now coexist in one Mastery blob: rot13 (line 2),\n  reversal (line 12), Atbash (line 13). Chunk edges split words (`-tliuq-d`, `m-uvim-66`, `yirwtv-78` -> `bridge-78`), so\n  per-line bigram guessing confused rev/Atbash on 20/63 sets. Parse rule (D13 solver `_joint`): decode the strict lines,\n  then try every assignment of {rot-N, reversal, Atbash} to the free lines, EACH TRANSFORM USED ONCE per blob, keep the\n  one whose joined text ends in a slug, best bigram. Whole-blob Atbash (single/TT) is in `lib.decode.decode_chunk`.\n  D14 added a FOURTH free codec, a second shift: Caesar +7 on line 14 (`u-pcf-67` -> `n-ivy-67`) beside rot13 on line 2.\n  Same rule, `lib.decode.FREE_CODECS` = rot(2) / rev(12) / atb(13) / caesar7(14); and rank a 3-word `adj-noun-noun-NN`\n  slug end above a 2-word one (set22 swapped rev/Atbash on lines 12/13 and still \"ended in a slug\": `g-voaafk|urtle-60`).\n  D15 added a FIFTH, ROT47 on line 15 (`@AA6C\\eg` -> `opper-68`: hyphen -> `\\`, digits -> `_`,`` ` ``,`a`-`h`, lowercase ->\n  `2`-`K`). It is NOT letters-only, so rot-N/Atbash/reversal can't produce it; registered with\n  `decode.register_free(\"rot47\", 15, ...)` (lib already listed rot47 as a SPECULATIVE free codec, day=None). 73/73 first try.\n  Rule for any new keyless printable-range shift: one `register_free(name, day, fn)` call, never a per-line special case.\n- **Layer count / carrier alphabet (D16)** -- single, TT and Mastery line 16 were all Morse whose decoded symbols are\n  ONLY `0-9A-F` (hex of the UTF-8 text): symbols split by ONE space, no `/` or 3-space word gaps (hex has no spaces), and\n  Morse is case-free, so the hex comes out UPPERCASE (`bytes.fromhex` takes it). Blob ~11 bytes per plaintext char\n  (1257 B single); a Mastery line-16 fragment = 14 symbols = 7 bytes (`-.... .---- --... -----` = `6170` = `ap`).\n  Parse rule: after any case/charset-collapsing outer decode (Morse, later NATO/Braille/tap...), if the output is\n  `(?:[0-9A-Fa-f]{2})+` -> `fromhex`; digits-only tokens -> decimal/octal char codes; otherwise feed it to\n  `decode_chunk` once more; accept only printable ASCII ending in a slug. In lib since D16: `decode_chunk`'s Morse\n  branch returns the hex-decoded text when the Morse output is pure hex pairs (gated `_max_day() >= 16`).\n  D17 = a SECOND hex carrier, Baconian: lowercase `a`/`b` 5-groups split by ONE space, only `aaaaa`..`abbbb` occur (values\n  0-15, so every group starts with `a`), TWO groups per byte, ~12 B per plaintext char (1295 B single); a Mastery line-17\n  fragment = 14 groups = 7 bytes. Read as Bacon-26 letters it gives ONLY `a`-`p` (`eogjgd...`) = the a-p hex alphabet\n  (nibble = ord-97) -- the answer-first worker went that way. Parse rule for any two-symbol group carrier: map the 2\n  symbols to 0/1, cut into fixed-width groups, then values all < 16 with an even count -> hex nibbles -> `fromhex`;\n  < 26 -> Bacon-26 letters (24-letter I=J/U=V if that reads better). In lib since the D17 night: `decode_chunk` strict\n  BACON-HEX branch (`[ab]{5}` groups, gated `_max_day() >= 17`), a `warmups_codecs.DAYS` D17 encoder, and the generic\n  `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` (any 2 symbols, widths 4-8, both polarities; also `bin4_dec`, `aphex_dec`, `tern_dec`,\n  `radix_dec`, `chuck_dec`, `dna_dec` for the sibling carriers). The D17 solver also has `register_free(\"baconhex\", 17, ...)`.\n- **Order / transposition (D18)** -- same character multiset, new order. Single/TT: the whole 109-110 B sentence\n  rail-fenced, 3 rails, offset 0 (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae...` = rail 0 then 1 then 2; one `!` `,` `:`, slug digits intact but\n  scattered). Mastery line 18: the 6-8 char slug-tail fragment rail-fenced ON ITS OWN (key restarts per line):\n  `p-pr5e6` -> `pper-56` (rail0 = chars 0,4; rail1 = 1,3,5; rail2 = 2,6). A 7-char rail-3 fragment still \"looks like\"\n  slug text, so no strict class claims it and best-rot/reversal would return garbage. Parse rule: for an anagram of the\n  crib (or a fragment no strict branch takes), try `warmups_codecs.rail_dec(t, r, off)` for r=3 first, then r 2..11 x\n  every offset, accept only a crib/slug hit (`Master:\\s*[a-z]+-[a-z]+-[a-z]+-\\d{2}$`); in Mastery try rail on each\n  unclaimed line inside the slug-gated joint search (lib `rail_solve`/`rail_free`, `register_free` with day 18). Any\n  future keyless permutation (columnar width, route, skip) is the same rule: enumerate the keys, gate on the crib/slug.\n- **Line wrapper** -- `NN<TAB>fragment` numbering; fragments split mid-word and join verbatim in number order.\n- **Set count** -- grows by 5 per day, 5N-2 (33 on day 7, ..., 78 on day 16, 83 x 17 lines on day 17, 88 x 18 on day 18); the solver must loop over whatever the contract lists, and its cost grows with sets x lines.\n- **Delivery shape** -- mastery bytes arrive as ONE zip saved under the first member's name (`blob-01.txt`; the contract has NO\n  `artifacts[]`, only a top-level `sha256` = the zip; same on days 7-9). Set 1's question names that same file: read the MEMBER, never decode the whole bundle.\n- **Payload only** -- TT/single differ only in the slug and length (day 7: 318 vs 333 bytes; day 8: 620 vs 621; day 9: 644 vs 650); same sentence and encoding.\n- **Fragment whitespace** -- payload spaces sit at fragment edges (day 9: 34/43 rot13 line-2 fragments end in a space, `pbqvat guvf `). Never `.strip()` the decoded text of a fragment before joining.\n\n## Recognition cues -> first move\n| What you see | First move |\n|---|---|\n| `[A-Za-z0-9+/]` ending `=`/`==`, length %4==0 | `base64 -d blob.txt` |\n| Letters/punctuation that look like English but aren't (\"Avpr jbex...\") | `tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'` (rot13). If that fails, try all 25 shifts scored by English words |\n| Only `[0-9a-f]`, even length, no spaces | `xxd -r -p blob.txt` |\n| Space-separated 8-char groups of 0/1 | `perl -lane 'print map chr oct \"0b$_\", @F'`, or python `int(x,2)` |\n| Space-separated 2-3 digit tokens, max <=127, unpadded (`32`, `78`) | decimal char codes: `python3 -c \"print(''.join(chr(int(t)) for t in open('blob.txt').read().split()))\"` |\n| 3-digit tokens, digits 0-7 only, zero-padded (`040`, `116`) | octal: same, with `int(t,8)` |\n| `%4e%69%63...` (every char incl. letters/spaces as lowercase `%xx`, no line end) | URL decode: `python3 -c \"import sys,urllib.parse;print(urllib.parse.unquote(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. Test `%[0-9a-f]{2}` BEFORE hex/b64 |\n| `&#78;&#105;&#99;...` (only `&...;` tokens, no line end) | HTML entities: `python3 -c \"import html,sys;print(html.unescape(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (covers `&#x..;` and named too) |\n| `\"\\u004e\\u0069...\"` (quoted, only `\\uXXXX`; prompt says \"escape\") | JSON string escapes: `python3 -c \"import json,sys;print(json.loads(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt`. `lib.decode.decode_chunk` also gets it (generic fallback, ASCII only) |\n| `A-Z2-7=` | `base32 -d` |\n| Readable letters backwards, a slug at the START (`56-ztrauq-...`), `retsaM emaG` (prompt: \"wrong end\") | `rev blob.txt` (or `[::-1]`); a line `NN-word-x` in a numbered blob is a reversed slug tail. `decode_chunk` does it since D12 |\n| English-shaped shifted letters, `Nhtl Thzaly:` = \"Game Master:\" +7 (prompt \"Caesar\", \"week\", \"seven\") | rot-7 (`tr 'A-Za-z' 'T-ZA-St-za-s'`); `decode_chunk`'s best rot-N finds it. In a Mastery blob it is a SECOND rot line -> `decode_chunked` (caesar7 in `FREE_CODECS` since D14) |\n| Letters, digits AND punctuation all scrambled, word-length spacing kept, `\\` where hyphens belong, `v2>6 |2DE6Ci` = \"Game Master:\" (prompt \"punctuation went for a spin\", \"Forty-Seven\") | ROT47: `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O' < blob.txt`; `lib.warmups_codecs.rot47`. Slug digits are rotated too (`\\he` -> `-96`), so no slug regex matches before decoding |\n| English-shaped words that no rot-N fixes, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` (prompt \"A met Z\", \"Opposite\") | Atbash: `tr 'A-Za-z' 'ZYXWVUTSRQPONMLKJIHGFEDCBAzyxwvutsrqponmlkjihgfedcba'`; `lib.decode.decode_chunk` picks it since D13 (bigram vs rot-N and reversal); `lib.ciphers.atbash` |\n| `[A-Za-z0-9_-]`, NO trailing `=`, length %4 != 0 (prompt: \"URL-friendly\") | unpadded base64url: `python3 -c \"import base64,sys;t=open(sys.argv[1]).read().strip();print(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len(t)%4)).decode())\" blob.txt` (`base64 -d` errors on it); `lib.decode.decode_chunk` handles it |\n| Only `a`/`b` (or `A`/`B`, `0`/`1`) in 5-groups, every group starting `a` (prompt \"a's and b's\", \"Bacon\", \"five\", \"sixteen\") | Bacon over hex: `python3 -c \"import sys;v=[int(w.replace('a','0').replace('b','1'),2) for w in open(sys.argv[1]).read().split()];print(bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%x for x in v)).decode())\" blob.txt`. Groups up to `bbabb` -> plain Bacon letters (`lib.warmups_codecs.bacon_dec`); other symbol pairs/widths -> `warmups_codecs.twosym_dec` |\n| Only `.` `-` and single spaces, symbols up to 5 long, all digits/`.`/`.-`/`-...`/`-.-.`/`-..`/`..-.` (prompt \"dot dot dot\", \"Dot Com\", \"sixteen\") | Morse -> hex: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(bytes.fromhex(w.morse_dec(open(sys.argv[1]).read()).replace(' ','')).decode())\" blob.txt`; `decode_chunk` does both layers since D16. Plain-text Morse (with `/` gaps, `-....-` hyphens) -> just `morse_dec` |\n| Readable letters in the wrong order, ~110 B, exactly one `!` `,` `:`, spaces scattered (`N kcnhwuTo,b saae`); prompt names rails/zigzag/fence/a number | rail fence: `python3 -c \"from lib import warmups_codecs as w,sys;print(w.rail_solve(open(sys.argv[1]).read()))\" blob.txt` (or `w.rail_dec(t,3,0)`; brute r 2-11 x offsets). Other anagram shapes -> `w.col_solve` (columnar/scytale), then route/skip |\n| Magic `PK\\x03\\x04` even if named `.txt` (mastery `blob-01.txt`) | `unzip -l`. It is a bundle of all sets `blob-01..NN.txt` |\n| Lines `01<TAB>...`, `02<TAB>...` | Strip the `NN\\t` prefix. Decode EACH line with its own encoding and concatenate without separators. Keep leading/trailing spaces |\n| Magic `1f8b`/`78`/`BZh`/`fd377a58` | gunzip / zlib / bzip2 / xz (in the toolbox. Not seen in the GM bytes yet) |\n| peel finds nothing | keyed/classical cipher: `lib.ciphers` (caesar_all, atbash, vigenere_crack, xor_crack, morse, base58, QP) |\nThe generic path is `lib.decode.peel(data, accept)` (BFS over encodings) plus the day solvers in `challenges/day0N/warmups-*/solver/solve.py`. Each later solver re-checks every earlier day's cases.\n\n## Day by day\n**Days 1-6** (base64, ROT13, hex, 8-bit binary, decimal, zero-padded octal): one layer each, solved in seconds-minutes.\nLessons that still matter: D1 mastery failed ~12x because same-named `blob.txt` downloads overwrote each other (match by\nsha256); D3 mastery timed out when single letters/hex counted as English (score 2+-letter words, cap peel at 0.6 s); D4 a\nrot-N must beat the UNSHIFTED text by a margin (\"er:\" -> \"an:\"); D6 a shell `read` loop dropped the last token (no trailing\nnewline), octal vs decimal = zero padding + lowercase share. Mastery from D2 on = ONE zip saved as `blob-01.txt`.\n\n**Days 7-9** (URL `%xx`, decimal HTML entities, quoted `\"\\u00XX\"` JSON string): one layer each, CORRECT in 18-53 s.\nLessons that still matter: D7 mastery attempt 1 decoded the WHOLE zip (bundle saved as `blob-01.txt`, a name match\nreturned the outer file) -> another set's slug fragment `py-storm-dune-80` -> incorrect; `lib.common.find_artifact` now\nreturns the same-named member. D8 a `str.replace` solver patch silently matched nothing (assert anchors). D9 lib's `\\u`\npath is ASCII-only (`\\u00e9`, `\\n`, `\\\"` would fall through to rot-N garbage; not in GM bytes yet). Strict whole-fragment\n`%xx` and entity branches live in `lib.decode.decode_chunk`.\n\n**Days 10-13** (base32; unpadded base64url; reversed sentence; Atbash): one layer each, CORRECT in 5-73 s.\nLessons that still matter: D11 `base64 -d`/strict len%4 b64 refused the unpadded blob and old `decode_chunk` fell through\nto rot-N garbage (re-pad first). D12 the carried-over solver answered in 5 s, but Mastery lost the timed attempt: a 28 s\ngeneric fallback, then 11/58 sets where rot-N \"decoded\" the reversed line 12 (`69-fove-n`). D13 the probe submitted a rot-N\nof Atbash text (`ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31`, rejected: gibberish words); Mastery 39/63 until free lines were assigned\nJOINTLY (`lib.decode._joint_free`, `FREE_CODECS`; each transform used once per blob).\n\n**Days 14-17** (Caesar +7; ROT47; Morse over hex; Bacon a/b 5-groups over hex): CORRECT in 4.5-39 s, first submit each day.\nLessons that still matter: D14 Mastery set22 went MISSING when a rev/Atbash swap ended in a 2-word slug -> `caesar7` in\n`FREE_CODECS`, 3-word slug ranked first. D15 ROT47 (`tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O'`) is beyond rot-N/Atbash/reversal;\n`register_free(\"rot47\", 15, ...)`. D16 Morse whose symbols are only `0-9A-F` -> `fromhex` (first two-layer warmup). D17\nBacon groups `aaaaa`-`abbbb` = nibbles; the answer-first worker read Bacon-26 letters (`a`-`p` only) then `ord-97` ->\n`fromhex` (~25 s). D17 Mastery (83 x 17) first run hit the harness's 60 s budget -- `answer()` ran the joint free-codec\nranker on every set even after `decode_chunked` had the slug; return-early fix -> 83 sets in 1.35 s.\n\n**Day 18: Off the Rails** (\"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\" -- rail fence, 3 rails, offset 0).\n- 109-byte blob, no newline, printable ASCII: `N kcnhwuTo,b saaeoemararm-o9iewr eoigti amp ...` = the crib sentence's\n  characters (one `!` `,` `:`) in a new order; triage preview alone shows it is an anagram. Rail 0 holds chars 0,4,8,...\n- Answer-first worker: one `python3 -I -c` that rail-decodes for r in 2..N x every offset and prints the reads; (3,0) gave\n  the sentence (offsets 2/3 give rotated copies `29Nice work...` -- reject those, the slug must END the text). Direct\n  `hctf submit`: start 13:00:09Z -> CORRECT 13:00:24Z (~15 s, first submit). lib already had `warmups_codecs.rail_solve`\n  (the atlas had listed rail fence as \"high: Off the Rails transposition\"), but the carried D17 solver never called it:\n  the prior-solver dry run answered 0/1. The duel worker's solve.py was verified at +41 s.\n- TT: the TT `start` at +19 s opened a 30 s clock (deadline 13:00:55Z) before any solver was verified; the timed run at\n  +47 s got `retry_variants_unavailable` -> untimed fallback, CORRECT (solve 0.17 s), no Hall of Fame.\n- Mastery 88 sets x 18 lines, window 105 s: the today-probe (`compound-today-d18-edge`, rail3 on the last line) caught that\n  `decode_chunked` misses line 18, so `_rail_lines` (each line in turn rail-decoded at (3,0),(2,0),(3,1..3), slug-gated)\n  was added before the attempt; 88/88 first try (solve 0.7 s, in_window 5.77 s).\n\n## GM answer conventions learned\n- Submit the slug ONLY, exactly as decoded (lowercase, hyphens, 2-digit number). Not the whole sentence and not \"Game Master: ...\". `trim` normalization only.\n- No correctly decoded warmups answer has been rejected (days 1-18; day 8's 40 and day 9's 45 slugs all accepted as decoded). The only `incorrect` (day 7 mastery set1) was a wrong value from decoding the whole bundle. The value is self-describing, so verify it against `answer_format.syntax` and submit.\n- Mastery ids are per set (`setN.message` style). Each question names `blob-0N.txt`, but the bytes may arrive as a zip member, as same-named `blob.txt` downloads, or de-duplicated (`blob_2.txt`). Map set -> file by sha256 from `contract[\"artifacts\"]` when present. When it is absent (day 7), open the zip and read member `blob-0N.txt`.\n- No GM-side label grammar in this lane: the answer is a derived VALUE, never a synthetic label.\n\n## Pitfalls that cost time\n- Mastery bytes missing: duplicate `blob.txt` downloads overwrote each other or were never fetched (day 1, about 12 failed attempts). If a set's bytes aren't on disk, it is a downloader problem. Don't spawn solver-hardening workers.\n- A `.txt` file that is really a ZIP (check the magic, not the extension). Prefer the exact inner member over the outer same-named container. Day 7 mastery lost an attempt to this: set 1 decoded the whole bundle. A slug that is a word suffix (`py-...`), or a set1 answer that doesn't come from its own last line, is the tell. Dry runs on renamed per-set copies do NOT reproduce the zip-named-`blob-01.txt` layout, so test on the raw delivery.\n- English scoring: count single letters and hex reads as English (day 3 timeout). Score common 2-letter words without a margin and a correct line gets rot-shifted (day 4). Require a margin over the unshifted text.\n- Encoding confusion: binary is also valid hex (try binary first), and decimal is also valid octal (use padding and case).\n- Fragments are split mid-word. Join with \"\" and keep payload whitespace. Joining with spaces corrupts the sentence and the slug.\n- No trailing newline: shell `read` loops lose the last token. Use python or `tr ' ' '\\n'` plus an explicit newline. Always regex-check the slug against the answer syntax.\n- Timed windows (5-9 s, with GM start/submit eating 3-5 s of it) leave no room for an LLM or a slow BFS. Keep every per-question decode under about 1 s with a hard BFS cap.\n- Solver patches made with `str.replace`/sed silently no-op when the anchor text doesn't match exactly (day 8 fallback). Assert\n  the anchor count or re-grep the file after patching.\n- Hand-testing escape decoders: in a ratchet session, a literal backslash-u sequence typed into a Bash/heredoc command\n  reached python ALREADY decoded (the tool layer unescaped it), so a test \"passed\" on plain text. Test on the artifact file\n  or build escapes with `chr(92)+'u%04x'`.\n- Unpadded base64: tools that demand `len%4==0` reject it or (worse) a length-gated classifier skips it and a fallback\n  returns plausible-looking rot-N text. Re-pad before decoding; `len%4==1` is the only impossible length.\n- Rot-N is the catch-all of `decode_chunk`, so ANY unrecognised plain-text transform (reversal on D12) comes back as\n  plausible-looking rot garbage, not as a failure. When a new day's line has no strict class, check `[::-1]` and other\n  plain transforms BEFORE trusting rot-N, and verify the slug's words read as English (`fove`, `ulev` don't).\n- Rejected D13 single `ovisx-tviskr-fbvecw-31`: a rot-N of Atbash text. Gibberish slug words = an unknown free-text\n  transform, not a GM mismatch -- don't submit a slug whose words aren't English.\n- Several free-text transforms in one blob (D13: rot13 / reversal / Atbash): decide them JOINTLY with each used once, not\n  per fragment -- short edge-split fragments score ambiguously and cost 24/63 Mastery sets.\n- A synthetic \"today's codec on line N\" check (compound-today cases) passing does NOT prove the joint assignment: D14's\n  real set22 still broke on edge-split rev/Atbash lines. Any fallback path must classify free lines exactly as\n  `lib.decode._free_line` does (rot / reversal / Atbash / new codec), and a slug check should demand the full 3-word shape.\n- The joint free-codec search grows with every new free codec (D15 Mastery solve 3.46 s with 5 free lines vs 0.2-0.3 s on\n  D8-D11). Prune by alphabet first: a ROT47 fragment has non-letters, so test that strict branch before the joint search.\n- A collapsed-alphabet outer layer (D16 Morse) decodes to something that is NOT English (`4E696365...`): a decoder that\n  stops after one layer returns no slug (or, scored as \"wordy\", a junk reading). When a decode yields only hex/digit\n  characters, it is an inner layer: decode again. Prompts with two cues (\"sixteen\" + \"dot dot dot\") = two layers.\n- lib has TWO Morse tables: `lib.ciphers.morse_decode` knows letters/digits only (drops `!` `,` `:` and the slug hyphen\n  `-....-`); `lib.warmups_codecs.morse_dec` knows punctuation. Use `morse_dec` for plain-text Morse.\n- A slow fallback (generic `peel` BFS per set) x 58 sets blows even a 30 s window: gate fallbacks on a hard per-set cap.\n- CHEAP PATH FIRST, RETURN EARLY (D17): a solver that builds `[decode_chunked(t)] + decode_candidates(t)` and then picks\n  pays the joint rot/rev/Atbash/... search on EVERY set. At 83 sets x 17 lines that passed 60 s and the Mastery run died\n  with no submission. Return the first candidate that yields a full slug; call the joint ranker only on a miss. Time the\n  real Mastery case (`hctf solver-test`) after every new free codec -- the cost grows with sets x lines x codecs.\n- A carrier decode that \"works\" can still be a middle layer: Bacon-26 letters of D17 came out as `a`-`p` only. An output\n  alphabet of exactly 16 symbols (0-9A-F, a-p, 16 words) = hex nibbles; decode again.\n- TRANSPOSITIONS (D18 rail fence): spaces are ciphertext -- a rail can start or end with a space, so strip only `\\r\\n`,\n  never `.strip()`. Wrong offsets give ROTATED readings that still contain the slug (`29Nice work ... dapper-summit-brook-`):\n  accept only a read that STARTS with `Nice work` / ends in a full `adj-noun-noun-NN`. Charset tells you nothing (it is\n  the plaintext's), the cue is \"readable letters, wrong order, exactly one `!` `,` `:`\".\n- A prebuilt lib codec is useless if the carried solver never routes to it (D18: `rail_solve` existed, prior solver 0/1).\n  First move on a new day: run lib's whole-blob candidates (`decode_chunk` + `warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES`/`*_solve`).\n- Mastery fragments of a keyed/transposition codec restart the key PER LINE and are short (6-8 chars on D18); only a\n  slug/crib gate over the joined text can confirm them. Test today's codec on the last Mastery line (compound-today probe).\n- Don't over-read the prompt: \"Again\" (day 6) was flavour, not a second layer. Decode what the bytes show.\n- Don't contact anything beyond the GM. The decoded sentence states the answer outright.\n","numLines":214,"startLine":1,"totalLines":214}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PkoenBuut6pKxYktik92Dy","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LnLAZng472vnm7QGMNFFEU","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Warmups coverage atlas\n2\t\n3\tLane: one `message` question over `blob.txt`. Plaintext is ALWAYS the fixed sentence\n4\t`Nice work decoding this warmup! To solve, submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <adj>-<noun>-<noun>-NN`\n5\t(87 chars + slug, ~105-115 bytes). One new keyless codec per day, named by a pun in the title; Mastery line k = day k's\n6\tcodec over a mid-word fragment (`NN<TAB>fragment`). D16-D17 were STACKED: a symbol carrier over HEX (D16 Morse, D17\n7\tBaconian a/b 5-groups whose values 0-15 are the nibbles), both cued by \"Sixteen\". D18 went back to ONE layer and opened\n8\ta new family, TRANSPOSITION: rail fence, 3 rails, offset 0 (\"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\" / \"Off the Rails\");\n9\tthe prompt's number was the key. Families run in streaks (D12-15 letter maps, D16-17 hex carriers), so the next days\n10\tmost likely bring other keyless permutations (columnar/scytale, route/Caesar box, skip, redefence, word order) or a\n11\ttransposition stacked on a seen codec. Snapshot 2026-10-18 (after D18; every gap from the D17 list is built).\n12\t\n13\tStatus legend: `seen Dn` = GM-confirmed day codec (`lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS`, `lib.decode.decode_chunk`/`FREE_CODECS`).\n14\t`prebuilt tgt:<id>` = speculative race module + fixtures from `tests/data/warmups_fixtures/TARGETS.json` (KEPT in\n15\t`.run/prebuild-state.json`). `prebuilt wc:<name>` = encoder/decoder pair in `lib.warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES` (used by\n16\t`infer`/`plaintext`/today-probe; free-text ones also `SPECULATIVE_FREE`). `prebuilt dec:<fn>` = `lib.decode` branch.\n17\t`gap` = nothing in lib (a standard tool may still decode it: chepy 7.x and CyberChef at `tools/npm-cyberchef` are\n18\tinstalled; `.venv` has `base58`, `pycipher`, `Crypto`; NOT installed: base45/base91/pybase62/pybase100/z85/unidecode).\n19\t\n20\t**Crib fact (all keyed rows):** the first 87 plaintext chars are known, so ANY fixed-key letter cipher leaks its key\n21\tfrom the blob alone: Vigenere/Beaufort/Gronsfeld/autokey key = (c - p) / (p - c) / (k - c) over the crib, period =\n22\tsmallest repeat; keyword substitution = the crib map (21 of 26 letters; only f j q y z unseen, slug-only).\n23\t\"Keyless?\" below therefore means \"decodable without outside info\": title pun OR crib.\n24\t\n25\t## Coverage table\n26\t\n27\t| # | concept | status | keyless? | recognition cues | standard decode | D19+ likelihood |\n28\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n29\t| 1 | base64 (std, padded) | seen D1 | yes | `A-Za-z0-9+/`, `=` pad, len%4==0, x1.33 | `base64 -d`; `base64.b64decode` | done (Mastery line 1) |\n30\t| 2 | ROT13 | seen D2 | yes | English word shapes, `Tnzr Znfgre:` | `tr A-Za-z N-ZA-Mn-za-m` | done |\n31\t| 3 | hex (base16) lowercase | seen D3 | yes | `[0-9a-f]` even len, x2 | `xxd -r -p`; `bytes.fromhex` | done |\n32\t| 4 | 8-bit binary, spaced | seen D4 | yes | `[01]{8}` groups | `int(x,2)` | done |\n33\t| 5 | decimal char codes | seen D5 | yes | tokens 32-126, no pad | `chr(int(x))` | done |\n34\t| 6 | octal char codes (3-digit) | seen D6 | yes | tokens `0[4-7][0-7]`/`1[0-7][0-7]` | `int(x,8)` | done |\n35\t| 7 | URL %XX (every byte) | seen D7 | yes | `%[0-9a-f]{2}` x3 len | `urllib.parse.unquote` | done |\n36\t| 8 | HTML decimal entities | seen D8 | yes | `&#\\d+;` only | `html.unescape` | done |\n37\t| 9 | JSON `\\uXXXX` quoted | seen D9 | yes | `\"N...\"` | `json.loads` | done |\n38\t| 10 | base32 RFC4648 padded | seen D10 | yes | `A-Z2-7`, `=` pad, len%8==0, x1.6 | `base32 -d` | done |\n39\t| 11 | base64url unpadded | seen D11 | yes | `-_` and/or len%4 in {2,3}, no `=` | `urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len%4))` | done |\n40\t| 12 | reversed text | seen D12 | yes | `:retsaM emaG`, slug first `NN-...` | `rev`; `[::-1]` | done |\n41\t| 13 | Atbash | seen D13 | yes | `Mrxv dlip`, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` | `tr` mirror; CyberChef Atbash | done |\n4