table, then `fromhex` | med |\n122\t| 94 | Morse as on/off timing units (`1`=dit, `111`=dah, `0`/`000`/`0000000` gaps) | prebuilt tgt:morse-timing-units | yes | one `[01]` run, many `111`, gaps of 1/3/7 zeros | split on `000`/`0000000` -> `.-` -> `morse_dec` | med: \"signal\"/\"telegraph\" sequel |\n123\t| 95 | DTMF keypad (16 symbols 0-9 A-D * #; tones `697+1209`) | prebuilt tgt:dtmf-keypad | yes | frequency pairs or keypad chars | (row,col) table | low-med: \"calling\"/\"dial tone\" |\n124\t| 96 | three-layer stacks (any D1-D17 codec x2 + new carrier) | prebuilt tgt:three-layer-peel | yes | intermediate output is base-N/hex/digits, not English | `lib.decode.peel` depth >= 3 under a 1 s cap | med: layering is the new trend |\n125\t| 97 | Baconian a/b 5-groups carrying hex nibbles (2 layers) | seen D17 | yes | only `a`/`b` + single spaces, groups `aaaaa`-`abbbb` (all start `a`), ~12 B per char; Bacon-26 reading = letters `a`-`p` only | a=0 b=1 -> `int(g,2)` -> `%x` -> `bytes.fromhex`; `decode_chunk` branch + DAYS D17 encoder | done (Mastery line 17) |\n126\t| 98 | generic two-symbol fixed-width groups (any 2 symbols, width 4-8, value -> nibble / letter / byte) | prebuilt wc:twosym (`twosym_dec`) | yes | 2-symbol alphabet (`.`/`-` excluded), constant group width, every group's top bit 0 | width from len, then values: <16 hex, <26 letter, 32-126 ASCII | high: D17 sequel (`0`/`1`, `X`/`O`, emoji, case) |\n127\t| 99 | 4-bit binary nibbles (`0100 1110`) carrying hex | prebuilt wc:bin4 (`bin4_dec`, + nibble swap) | yes | `[01]{4}` groups only | `int(g,2)` -> `%x` -> `fromhex` | high: \"sixteen\"/\"four\" hex carrier |\n128\t| 100 | a-p letter hex (nibble = ord-97, \"hexdump letters\") | prebuilt wc:aphex (`aphex_dec`, `aphex_over_dec`) | yes | only `a`-`p`, even length, ~2 letters/char | `bytes.fromhex(''.join('%x'%(ord(c)-97) ...))` | high: D17's middle layer as its own day |\n129\t| 101 | base-3/4/5/7 char codes (quaternary `1032`, ternary `10012`) | prebuilt wc:radix (`radix_dec`) | yes | fixed-width digit groups over a small digit set | `chr(int(g,b))`; CyberChef From Charcode base | med |\n130\t| 102 | w-bit groups as base-2^w INDEX (5-bit -> base32 `A-Z2-7`, 6-bit -> base64 sextets, 3-bit -> octal digits) | prebuilt tgt:bitgroup-base-index | yes | two-symbol groups of width 5/6 with values up to 31/63 (D17 used only 0-15) | value -> alphabet char, then base32/base64 decode | high: \"Thirty-Two\"/\"Sixty-Four\" + carrier pun |\n131\t| 103 | unknown 16-token alphabet over hex, mapped by the CRIB (words, emoji, glyphs, colours) | prebuilt tgt:crib-token-hex | yes (crib) | exactly <=16 distinct tokens, even count, ~2 tokens per char | `Nice work` = `4e 69 63 65 20 77 6f 72 6b` fixes the token->nibble map | high: any new \"sixteen\" dress |\n132\t| 104 | old outer transform over an old base string (reversed/rot13/Atbash/ROT47 base64, reversed hex) | prebuilt tgt:free-over-strict | yes | base64-ish alphabet that refuses to decode; hex with `u-z`; `==` at the START | undo the free transform, then decode | med-high: stacking two seen codecs |\n133\t| 105 | I Ching hexagrams (U+4DC0-4DFF, 64) / trigrams (U+2630-2637, 8) as base64 sextets / octal | prebuilt tgt:iching-hexagram | yes | `䷀䷁䷂` or `☰☱☲` only | index = cp - 0x4DC0 -> base64 alphabet | med: \"Sixty-Four\" pun |\n134\t| 106 | whitespace stego (spaces/tabs = bits, SNOW trailing whitespace) | prebuilt tgt:whitespace-stego | yes | blob or line ends of only ` ` and TAB | space=0 tab=1, 8-bit (or 4-bit hex) | med: \"invisible\"/\"blank\" pun |\n135\t| 107 | seven-segment display codes for hex digits 0-F | prebuilt tgt:seven-segment-hex | yes | 7-bit masks (`0x3f 0x06`...) or segment-letter sets (`abcdef`, `bc`) | std 7-seg table -> hex digit -> fromhex | med: \"Sixteen\"/\"segments\" pun |\n136\t| 108 | address notations (IPv4 dotted-decimal quads, IPv6 groups, MAC, UUID) carrying bytes | prebuilt tgt:address-notation | yes | `78.105.99.101 32.119...`, `4e69:6365:...` | split -> int / hex -> bytes | med: \"address\"/\"network\" pun |\n137\t| 109 | CSS/HTML colour codes `#4E6963` / `rgb(78,105,99)` carrying bytes | prebuilt tgt:css-color-hex | yes | only `#xxxxxx` tokens, 3 bytes each | strip `#` -> fromhex | med: \"colours\"/\"palette\" pun |\n138\t| 110 | regional-indicator letters (U+1F1E6-1F1FF, flag emoji) | prebuilt tgt:regional-indicator | yes | `🇳🇮🇨🇪` pairs render as flags | cp - 0x1F1E6 -> A-Z (case lost: lowercase) | low-med: \"flags\" pun |\n139\t| 111 | ordered glyph alphabets as radix digits (dice ⚀-⚅, card suits, chess, zodiac) | prebuilt tgt:glyph-radix | yes | one Unicode block, <=12 distinct glyphs, fixed-width groups | glyph -> index -> `radix_dec` rules | low-med |\n140\t| 112 | skip / decimation cipher (read every k-th char mod n) | gap (gap skip-decimation) | yes (brute k, start) | anagram; no rail/columnar read | `c[i]=p[(s+i*k)%n]`, gcd(k,n)=1, invert | high: \"skip/hop/every Nth\" pun |\n141\t| 113 | redefence (rail fence, rails read in key order) | gap (gap redefence) | yes (brute r! for r<=6) / title digits | anagram; rail reads come out in shuffled blocks | permute rails by key, then `rail_dec` | high: D18 sequel |\n142\t| 114 | transposition stacked on a strict encoding (rail/columnar/reverse of a base64/hex/Morse STRING, or base64 of a rail-fenced text) | gap (gap transposition-over-strict) | yes | strict charset that refuses to decode, or decodes to an anagram | undo the outer layer, then the inner (peel with transposition pre-steps) | high: D16-17 stacking x D18 family |\n143\t| 115 | word-level transposition (whole words rail-fenced/columnar/shuffled; per-word letter rail) | gap (gap word-transposition) | yes (brute) | every word English, order wrong; slug token intact | permute tokens by rail/columnar keys, crib gate | med-high |\n144\t| 116 | riffle / perfect shuffle (in/out shuffle x k), odd-even split | gap (gap riffle-shuffle) | yes (brute k) | anagram; 2-rail reads half right | apply inverse shuffle k times | med: \"deck\"/\"shuffle\" pun |\n145\t| 117 | double columnar / Myszkowski / keyed columnar with title word | prebuilt tgt:columnar (single key only); gap double-columnar-myszkowski | title | anagram; no keyless read works | `col_dec` twice; Myszkowski ties read together | med |\n146\t| 118 | transposition + letter map stacked (rail of ROT13/Atbash/Caesar text, or vice versa) | gap (gap transposition-plus-letter-map) | yes | letters of the crib multiset NOT matching, but a rot/Atbash of it does | multiset test after each letter map, then transposition sweep | med-high: two seen families stacked |\n147\t\n148\t## Recognition cues (worker quick-reference)\n149\t\n150\t```\n151\tKnown plaintext: \"Nice work decoding this warmup! To solve, submit this exact answer to the Game Master: a-b-c-NN\"\n152\t word lengths: 4 4 8 4 6! 2 5, 6 4 5 6 2 3 4 6: slug   (exactly one \"!\", one \",\", one \":\")\n153\t crib letters cover all but f j q y z; slug is ALWAYS lowercase -> lowercase case-losing decodes before submit\n154\tSTEP 1 - lib first: python3 -c \"from lib import decode,warmups_codecs as w;t=open('blob.txt').read();\n155\t  print(decode.decode_chunk(t)); print([n for n,e,d in w.CANDIDATES if d and w.SLUG.search(w.safe(d,t.strip()) or '')])\"\n156\tSTEP 2 - charset fingerprint (python: sorted(set(t)), len(t), len(t)/110):\n157\t [01] 8/7-groups .................. binary (D4); 4-groups -> nibbles->hex; 5-groups -> Bacon/Baudot; 0-blocks -> Chuck Norris\n158\t [0-7] 3-digit ................... octal (D6);   [0-9] 32-126 tokens -> decimal (D5); 1-26 dashed -> A1Z26\n159\t digit pairs, digits 1-5 only ..... Polybius / tap-code-digits;  . or x knock groups -> tap code\n160\t digit runs of 2-9 repeats ........ multitap (44 33 555); single digits 2-9 per letter -> T9\n161\t [0-9a-f] even, x2 ................ hex (D3); c1-e9/81-a9 + 40 -> EBCDIC cp037; 00 every 2nd byte -> UTF-16\n162\t . - only (+ / or 3 spaces) ....... Morse (D16: output only 0-9A-F -> hex layer; digits -> char codes); 1/111 runs -> timing\n163\t a/b (A/B) in 5s .................. Baconian (D17: all groups start `a` -> values 0-15 = HEX nibbles, 2 per byte);\n164\t                                    values up to 25 -> letters; odd casing in cover text -> Bacon via case\n165\t ANY 16-symbol output (0-9A-F, a-p only, 16 words) after a carrier = hex nibbles -> fromhex (D16, D17);\n166\t   unknown 16 tokens -> map them by the crib hex `4e 69 63 65 20 77 6f 72 6b`. lib: twosym/bin4/aphex/tern/radix_dec\n167\t 2-symbol groups of width 5/6 with values >15 -> base32/base64 INDEX;  ䷀.. hexagrams -> base64;  only space/TAB -> bits\n168\t dotted quads / #RRGGBB / 4e69:6365 . IPv4 / colour / IPv6 bytes;  base64-ish that won't decode -> undo rev/rot13/atbash 1st\n169\t only A D F G X (V) ............... ADFGX / ADFGVX (keyed: try title word + crib)\n170\t only A C G T ..................... DNA 2-bit / codon\n171\t %XX .............................. URL (D7);  &#..; -> HTML (D8);  \\uXXXX -> JSON (D9);  \\xNN -> C escape\n172\t U+XXXX / %uXXXX / \\U0000XXXX ..... Unicode code-point notation -> chr(int(h,16))\n173\t =XX tokens / soft \"=\\n\" .......... quoted-printable;  =?UTF-8?B?..?= -> MIME header\n174\t A-Z2-7 (=) ....................... base32 (D10);  0-9A-V -> base32hex\n175\t A-Za-z0-9+/ (=), len%4==0 ........ base64 (D1); starts H4sI -> gzip, eJ -> zlib inside (lib.decode.peel)\n176\t A-Za-z0-9-_ no = ................. base64url (D11)\n177\t [0-9A-Za-z] only, no pad ......... base62 (x1.34) / base36 if one case (x1.55)\n178\t no 0 O I l + / = ................. base58 (x1.37)\n179\t 0-9A-Z plus space $%*+-./: ....... base45 (x1.5)\n180\t ! .. u (+z), maybe <~ ~> ......... ascii85;  RFC1924 set (no quotes , . / : [ \\ ]) -> base85; .-:+=^!/*?&<>()[]{}@%$# -> Z85\n181\t ~all printable, no ' - \\ ......... basE91 (x1.23);  ! .. } minus \" ` -> base92\n182\t begin 644 / lines start M or h ... uuencode / xxencode\n183\t xn-- labels ...................... punycode/IDNA\n184\tNON-ASCII:\n185\t U+2800-28FF ⠝⠊⠉⠑ ................. Braille (⠼ = number sign)\n186\t fullwidth Ｎｉｃｅ / math 𝐍𝐢𝐜𝐞 / ⓝ .. NFKC normalize\n187\t ɐ q ɔ p ǝ ɟ ƃ ɥ ᴉ ʞ ɯ ɹ ʇ ʌ ʍ ʎ ...... upside-down (chepy from_upside_down, then maybe [::-1])\n188\t CJK U+7Cxx, spaces kept .......... ROT8000 (chepy rot_8000)\n189\t dense CJK/Hangul, no spaces ...... base65536/base2048;  all emoji 1 per byte -> base100\n190\t invisible U+200B/C/D/2060/FEFF ... zero-width stego (count them: len(t) vs visible)\n191\t ASCII-looking with odd bytes ..... homoglyphs (Cyrillic а е о р с х)\n192\tSAME LENGTH, LETTERS ONLY CHANGE (word lengths 4 4 8 4 6! ... kept):\n193\t best rot-N English ............... Caesar (D2 rot13, D14 +7); digits also shifted by 5 -> ROT18/ROT5\n194\t mirror a<->z ..................... Atbash (D13)\n195\t crib c-p constant ratio .......... affine (lib _fit_affine); constant per position mod k -> Vigenere key = (c-p)\n196\t crib c+p periodic ................ Beaufort; no period but primer then plaintext -> autokey\n197\t crib map consistent, not affine .. keyword substitution (21 letters from crib)\n198\t neighbour keys (Muvr eptl) ....... QWERTY shift;  ;,.' in words -> Dvorak;  ALL printables shift -> ROT47 (D15)/ROT8000\n199\tSAME CHARACTER MULTISET (anagram: one ! , :, slug digits intact) = TRANSPOSITION (D12 reverse, D18 rail 3):\n200\t rail fence (D18: number in prompt = rails; w.rail_solve) | redefence | scytale/columnar (w.col_solve) | route/Caesar box\n201\t | skip k | riffle | word order. Strip only \\r\\n (spaces are ciphertext); accept only text STARTING `Nice work`\n202\tWORDS: Alfa Bravo -> NATO;  -ay endings -> Pig Latin;  N1c3 w0rk -> leet;  Ook./+-<>[] -> esoteric program\n203\tTitle pun -> key: a cipher name + a number/word in the prompt = the key (D18 \"Three rails\"); verify on the crib.\n204\tMastery: line k = day k's codec; keyed/transposed lines restart the key PER LINE (D18 line 18 = 6-8 char rail-3 slug\n205\t tail). STACKS (D16-17): output hex/digits/b32/a-p -> decode AGAIN. Solver: return on the first full slug; slow fallbacks only on a miss.\n206\t```\n207\t\n208\t## Readiness gaps\n209\t\n210\t```json\n211\t[\n212\t  {\"id\": \"transposition-sweep\", \"concept\": \"one keyless-transposition entry point: every permutation family in lib (reverse, rail r x offset, redefence, columnar/scytale widths, route, skip, riffle, word order), each also composed with reversal, crib-gated\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"D18 opened the transposition family (rail 3). Add warmups_codecs.transposition_solve(blob): multiset test against crib+slug first (cheap reject), then enumerate keys family by family under a 0.5 s cap, accept only text that STARTS 'Nice work' and ends in a 3-word slug (rotated rail offsets contain the slug too). Fragment mode for Mastery (slug-tail gate, key restarts per line) and a register_free hook so a new day's transposition joins the joint search with one call. Strip only CR/LF: spaces are ciphertext. Fixtures: each family on the 110-B sentence + 6-8 char fragments.\"},\n213\t  {\"id\": \"route-caesar-box\", \"concept\": \"route / Caesar box / spiral / boustrophedon (snake) / diagonal zigzag reads of a w x h grid\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Row 55. For every w,h with w*h >= len (pad 'x'/' '/'_' or none): write by rows, read by columns / spiral cw+ccw from each corner / snake rows / snake columns / diagonal (JPEG-style zigzag - fits the 'zigzag' vocabulary). Invert each read; crib-gated. CyberChef 'Caesar Box Cipher' = column read of a square-ish box. Fixtures single + fragment.\"},\n214\t  {\"id\": \"skip-decimation\", \"concept\": \"skip cipher / decimation: ciphertext = every k-th plaintext char mod n\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"c[i] = p[(s + i*k) % n] for gcd(k,n)=1 (dcode 'Skip cipher'); brute k in 2..n-1, s in 0..n-1 with the crib prefix check (n~110 -> ~12k cheap tries; check the first 9 chars before building the rest). Also the non-coprime variant that wraps with +1 per lap. Pun cues: skip, hop, leap, every Nth, stride, step.\"},\n215\t  {\"id\": \"redefence\", \"concept\": \"redefence: rail fence whose rails are emitted in a key order (rails permuted), optional offset\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Natural D18 sequel. r<=7 rails: brute r! orders x offsets (7! x 12 too many at full decode: check the crib prefix from the first rail positions only), key also from digits/words in the prompt ('3-1-2', 'THREE'). Reuse rail_rows to get rail lengths, slice the ciphertext in key order, then zigzag-read. Fixtures r=3..5.\"},\n216\t  {\"id\": \"transposition-over-strict\", \"concept\": \"a transposition stacked on a seen strict encoding: rail/columnar/reverse of a base64/hex/base32/Morse STRING, or base64/hex of a rail-fenced sentence\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"D16-17 stacked two layers; D18 introduced transposition. Cues: base64/hex/b32 alphabet that refuses to decode or has '=' mid-string; Morse/hex that decodes to an anagram of the crib. Add to peel: after a strict decode yields an anagram -> transposition_sweep; before a strict decode that fails -> try rail/columnar/rev on the encoded string (gate: strict decode must then succeed AND read crib). Under the 1 s cap.\"},\n217\t  {\"id\": \"word-transposition\", \"concept\": \"word-level transposition: whole words rail-fenced / columnar / shuffled by a key, or each word's letters rail-fenced on their own\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Cue: every token is an English word (or an anagram of one), sentence order wrong, slug token intact or anagrammed. Tokens = space-split (16 crib words + slug); apply the transposition_sweep families at token level; per-word mode applies rail/reverse inside each word. Crib-gated.\"},\n218\t  {\"id\": \"case-and-digit-loss\", \"concept\": \"case-losing / digit-losing codecs (Morse, NATO, Polybius, tap, Bacon, A1Z26, T9)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"Output must be lowercased; A1Z26/Polybius/Bacon/tap have no digit symbols, so slug digits are passed through literally or spelled - the decoder must keep non-alphabet tokens verbatim. decode_chunk Morse returns UPPERCASE. D16 confirmed the collapse direction: Morse output was UPPERCASE hex; lowercase only the FINAL text, and test the collapsed output for an inner hex/digit layer before lowercasing. D17: Bacon is case-free too and its letter reading (a-p) was a middle layer - same rule. The D17-night carrier decoders (twosym/bin4/aphex/tern/radix/chuck/dna) return lowercase-ready text; keep the rule for any new table carrier.\"},\n219\t  {\"id\": \"transposition-mastery-joint\", \"concept\": \"short Mastery fragments under a transposition (6-8 chars, key restarts per line)\", \"priority\": 1, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"D18 line 18 ('p-pr5e6' -> 'pper-56') has no charset cue; decode_chunked missed it and the solver added a slug-gated per-line rail pass. Any future transposition day: register its decoder as a free codec with its day number so _joint_free tries it on each unclaimed line; a 6-char fragment has few distinct rail/columnar reads, so the joined-text slug gate is the only arbiter.\"},\n220\t  {\"id\": \"transposition-plus-letter-map\", \"concept\": \"transposition stacked with a seen letter map (rail fence of ROT13/Atbash/Caesar+7/ROT47 text, either order)\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Cue: crib multiset test fails, but after rot13/atbash/rot-N/rot47 the multiset matches (letter maps commute with transpositions, so order does not matter for decoding). Run each FREE_CODECS map, re-test the multiset, then transposition_sweep. Fixtures: rail3(rot13), atbash(rail3), rail3(rot47).\"},\n221\t  {\"id\": \"riffle-shuffle\", \"concept\": \"perfect riffle shuffle (in/out shuffle repeated k times), odd/even interleave\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"'deck'/'shuffle'/'cards' pun. Out-shuffle = interleave halves; in-shuffle starts with the second half; repeat k in 1..order (order of the permutation <= n). Decode = inverse permutation applied k times; crib-gated. Rail 2 offset 0 = one un-shuffle; make sure the sweep reports the right family name.\"},\n222\t  {\"id\": \"double-columnar-myszkowski\", \"concept\": \"double columnar transposition and Myszkowski (repeated key letters read together), keys from title/prompt words\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"tgt:columnar covers single keyless widths + one title key. Add: same key twice, two different prompt words, Myszkowski tie rule; keys = every word/number of title+prompt and their uppercase/no-space forms; crib-gated; cap 0.5 s.\"},\n223\t  {\"id\": \"affine-free-strict\", \"concept\": \"affine as a GM-confirmed-ready free codec\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"Already prebuilt (wc:affine + SPECULATIVE_FREE + _fit_affine). Risk is only bigram ranking vs rot-N/Atbash in Mastery joint assignment; confirm ratchet path registers it with a day number on first sight.\"},\n224\t  {\"id\": \"chepy-cyberchef-cue\", \"concept\": \"standard-tool fallback for any unseen encoding\", \"priority\": 2, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"Workers should know chepy (venv) has from_base45/58/62/85/91/92/65536, rot_8000, from_upside_down, decode_zero_width, from_fullwidth, from_bacon, from_braille, from_nato, sms_decode_multitap, vigenere_decode, bifid_decode; CyberChef ops at tools/npm-cyberchef/node_modules/cyberchef/src/core/operations (run via the *_cyberchef.mjs pattern).\"},\n225\t  {\"id\": \"bifid-std-square\", \"concept\": \"Bifid / Trifid with a STANDARD (keyless) square, period = full length or 5..10\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"prebuild\", \"detail\": \"Row 75. Fractionation + transposition; keyless when the square is the plain A-Z (I=J) alphabet. Letters only change (spaces/punct kept or dropped), length kept, crib letters not a monoalphabetic map. pycipher Bifid/Delastelle; brute period; crib-gated. Trifid uses a 27-char alphabet (+ '.').\"},\n226\t  {\"id\": \"base92\", \"concept\": \"base92\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"chepy from_base92 / CyberChef From Base92 decode it; cue = printable minus double-quote and backtick.\"},\n227\t  {\"id\": \"base65536\", \"concept\": \"base65536 / base2048 / base32768\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"chepy from_base65536; others need pip (base2048) - install only if seen.\"},\n228\t  {\"id\": \"bifid-adfgvx\", \"concept\": \"Bifid / Trifid / ADFGX / ADFGVX / Nihilist\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"pycipher implements all; key from title; ADFGX cue is the 5/6-letter alphabet.\"},\n229\t  {\"id\": \"mime-encoded-word\", \"concept\": \"MIME encoded-word headers\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"email.header.decode_header handles =?charset?B|Q?...?=.\"},\n230\t  {\"id\": \"esoteric\", \"concept\": \"Brainfuck / Ook! (Chuck Norris unary is prebuilt: chuck_dec)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"tool\", \"detail\": \"No bf interpreter installed; a 20-line stdlib interpreter or pip 'brainfuck-interpreter' if one ever appears.\"},\n231\t  {\"id\": \"misc-tables\", \"concept\": \"chemical symbols, Cetacean, Citrix CTX1 (DNA/codon, Roman, BCD, Gray, Kenny are prebuilt in warmups_codecs)\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"note\", \"detail\": \"Recognition cues only; each is a lookup table the worker can write in one turn; CyberChef has From BCD and Cetacean Cipher Decode.\"},\n232\t  {\"id\": \"homoglyph\", \"concept\": \"homoglyph (Cyrillic/Greek look-alike) substitution\", \"priority\": 3, \"action\": \"tool\", \"detail\": \"unidecode/confusables not installed; NFKC does not fold Cyrillic. Install unidecode only if a day uses it.\"}\n233\t]\n234\t```\n235\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3998553-dd92-4aba-82dd-28852dfc57a2","uuid":"21d55f7f-dae1-4f12-934e-0eec1b1bd526","timestamp":"2026-10-19T13:03:47.594Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/testpwn/huntress-2026/reference/coverage/warmups.md","content":"# Warmups coverage atlas\n\nLane: one `message` question over `blob.txt`. Plaintext is ALWAYS the fixed sentence\n`Nice work decoding this warmup! To solve, submit this exact answer to the Game Master: <adj>-<noun>-<noun>-NN`\n(87 chars + slug, ~105-115 bytes). One new keyless codec per day, named by a pun in the title; Mastery line k = day k's\ncodec over a mid-word fragment (`NN<TAB>fragment`). D16-D17 were STACKED: a symbol carrier over HEX (D16 Morse, D17\nBaconian a/b 5-groups whose values 0-15 are the nibbles), both cued by \"Sixteen\". D18 went back to ONE layer and opened\na new family, TRANSPOSITION: rail fence, 3 rails, offset 0 (\"Three rails. One very zigzag commute.\" / \"Off the Rails\");\nthe prompt's number was the key. Families run in streaks (D12-15 letter maps, D16-17 hex carriers), so the next days\nmost likely bring other keyless permutations (columnar/scytale, route/Caesar box, skip, redefence, word order) or a\ntransposition stacked on a seen codec. Snapshot 2026-10-18 (after D18; every gap from the D17 list is built).\n\nStatus legend: `seen Dn` = GM-confirmed day codec (`lib.warmups_codecs.DAYS`, `lib.decode.decode_chunk`/`FREE_CODECS`).\n`prebuilt tgt:<id>` = speculative race module + fixtures from `tests/data/warmups_fixtures/TARGETS.json` (KEPT in\n`.run/prebuild-state.json`). `prebuilt wc:<name>` = encoder/decoder pair in `lib.warmups_codecs.CANDIDATES` (used by\n`infer`/`plaintext`/today-probe; free-text ones also `SPECULATIVE_FREE`). `prebuilt dec:<fn>` = `lib.decode` branch.\n`gap` = nothing in lib (a standard tool may still decode it: chepy 7.x and CyberChef at `tools/npm-cyberchef` are\ninstalled; `.venv` has `base58`, `pycipher`, `Crypto`; NOT installed: base45/base91/pybase62/pybase100/z85/unidecode).\n\n**Crib fact (all keyed rows):** the first 87 plaintext chars are known, so ANY fixed-key letter cipher leaks its key\nfrom the blob alone: Vigenere/Beaufort/Gronsfeld/autokey key = (c - p) / (p - c) / (k - c) over the crib, period =\nsmallest repeat; keyword substitution = the crib map (21 of 26 letters; only f j q y z unseen, slug-only).\n\"Keyless?\" below therefore means \"decodable without outside info\": title pun OR crib.\n\n## Coverage table\n\n| # | concept | status | keyless? | recognition cues | standard decode | D19+ likelihood |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | base64 (std, padded) | seen D1 | yes | `A-Za-z0-9+/`, `=` pad, len%4==0, x1.33 | `base64 -d`; `base64.b64decode` | done (Mastery line 1) |\n| 2 | ROT13 | seen D2 | yes | English word shapes, `Tnzr Znfgre:` | `tr A-Za-z N-ZA-Mn-za-m` | done |\n| 3 | hex (base16) lowercase | seen D3 | yes | `[0-9a-f]` even len, x2 | `xxd -r -p`; `bytes.fromhex` | done |\n| 4 | 8-bit binary, spaced | seen D4 | yes | `[01]{8}` groups | `int(x,2)` | done |\n| 5 | decimal char codes | seen D5 | yes | tokens 32-126, no pad | `chr(int(x))` | done |\n| 6 | octal char codes (3-digit) | seen D6 | yes | tokens `0[4-7][0-7]`/`1[0-7][0-7]` | `int(x,8)` | done |\n| 7 | URL %XX (every byte) | seen D7 | yes | `%[0-9a-f]{2}` x3 len | `urllib.parse.unquote` | done |\n| 8 | HTML decimal entities | seen D8 | yes | `&#\\d+;` only | `html.unescape` | done |\n| 9 | JSON `\\uXXXX` quoted | seen D9 | yes | `\"N...\"` | `json.loads` | done |\n| 10 | base32 RFC4648 padded | seen D10 | yes | `A-Z2-7`, `=` pad, len%8==0, x1.6 | `base32 -d` | done |\n| 11 | base64url unpadded | seen D11 | yes | `-_` and/or len%4 in {2,3}, no `=` | `urlsafe_b64decode(t+'='*(-len%4))` | done |\n| 12 | reversed text | seen D12 | yes | `:retsaM emaG`, slug first `NN-...` | `rev`; `[::-1]` | done |\n| 13 | Atbash | seen D13 | yes | `Mrxv dlip`, `Gsv Tznv Nzhgvi:` | `tr` mirror; CyberChef Atbash | done |\n| 14 | Caesar +7 | seen D14 | yes | `Upjl dvyr`, best rot-N = 19 | rot-7 | done |\n| 15 | ROT47 | seen D15 | yes | all printable shifted, spaces kept, `|2DE6Ci` | `tr '!-~' 'P-~!-O'` | done |\n| 16 | hex variants (UPPER, spaced, `0x`, `:`, `\\x`) | prebuilt wc:hex-upper/-spaced/-0x/-colon, escape\\x | yes | separators/prefixes around hex pairs | `bytes.fromhex(re.sub(...))` | med: cheap \"same family, new dress\" day |\n| 17 | binary variants (7-bit, no spaces) | prebuilt wc:binary7, binary8-nospace | yes | 7-char groups / one 0-1 run | `int(...,2)` | low: D4 did binary |\n| 18 | binary LSB-first / bit-reversed | gap | yes | binary groups decoding to junk MSB-first | `basenc --base2lsbf -d`; `int(x[::-1],2)` | low |\n| 19 | decimal/octal variants (comma, padded, `\\NNN`) | prebuilt wc:decimal-comma*, decimal3, octal, octal-backslash | yes | separators, leading zeros | `int` | low |\n| 20 | charcodes shifted (+N) / other radix (base-4/5/7) | gap | yes (offset from crib) | numeric tokens not in 32-126; 4-digit base-4 | CyberChef From Charcode (base 2-36) | low |\n| 21 | HTML hex entities / named | prebuilt wc:html&#xh;, html&#n;-alnum | yes | `&#x4e;`, `&amp;` | `html.unescape` | low: D8 family |\n| 22 | U+XXXX / `\\U0000XXXX` / CSS `\\4e ` / `%u004e` | prebuilt tgt:unicode-codepoint-notation | yes | `U+004E` tokens; `\\U` 8-hex; `%u` | regex + `chr(int(h,16))`; chepy unicode_to_str | med: \"escape\" family sibling |\n| 23 | base32hex / base32 nopad / lower | prebuilt wc:base32hex, base32-nopad, base32-lower | yes | `0-9A-V`; no `=`; lowercase | `base64.b32hexdecode` | low |\n| 24 | base64 nopad / url padded | prebuilt wc:base64-nopad, base64url | yes | as D1/D11 | pad + decode | low |\n| 25 | base36 | prebuilt wc:base36 | yes | `[0-9a-z]` one case, x~1.55 | `int(t,36).to_bytes` | low |\n| 26 | base45 (RFC 9285) | prebuilt tgt:base45 | yes | `0-9A-Z $%*+-./:`, len%3 in {0,2} | chepy from_base45; CyberChef From Base45 | med: \"QR\"/\"Forty-Five\" pun |\n| 27 | base58 (bitcoin) | prebuilt wc:base58 | yes | no `0OIl+/=`, x~1.37 | `.venv` base58; `base